description:
The London Borough of Waltham Forest is undertaking preliminary market engagement to inform the future commissioning of Children's Short Break Services for disabled children and young people. The Council is seeking to establish a sustainable, flexible and family-centred model that supports its statutory responsibilities whilst enabling the Short Breaks offer to evolve in response to the needs, aspirations and lived experience of children, young people and families. This engagement forms part of a wider co-production programme involving children, young people, parent carers, providers and partner organisations. We are keen to understand the market's views on how we can collectively develop a strong, diverse and innovative Short Breaks offer for Waltham Forest over the coming years. 
The Council welcomes participation from: 
• Existing Short Break providers; 
• Specialist SEND providers; 
• Voluntary and community sector organisations; 
• Social enterprises; 
• Leisure and recreation providers; 
• Arts, culture and creative organisations; 
• Providers delivering sports, outdoor learning and adventure activities; 
• Local businesses and community organisations; 
• Potential new market entrants.

The London Borough of Waltham Forest is undertaking preliminary market engagement to inform the future commissioning of Children's Short Break Services for disabled children and young people. The Council is seeking to establish a sustainable, flexible and family-centred model that supports its statutory responsibilities whilst enabling the Short Breaks offer to evolve in response to the needs, aspirations and lived experience of children, young people and families. This engagement forms part of a wider co-production programme involving children, young people, parent carers, providers and partner organisations. We are keen to understand the market's views on how we can collectively develop a strong, diverse and innovative Short Breaks offer for Waltham Forest over the coming years. 
The Council welcomes participation from: 
• Existing Short Break providers; 
• Specialist SEND providers; 
• Voluntary and community sector organisations; 
• Social enterprises; 
• Leisure and recreation providers; 
• Arts, culture and creative organisations; 
• Providers delivering sports, outdoor learning and adventure activities; 
• Local businesses and community organisations; 
• Potential new market entrants.

The Contract is for the provision of services for Enhanced Inclusion Support Pathways In the London Borough of Waltham Forest and aims to:

•	Keep more children and young people safe, well, resilient and independent, and support them to get the most from educational opportunities in mainstream schools, wherever possible;
•	Build capacity in Waltham Forest schools and settings to respond to social, emotional and mental health (SEMH) needs of children, to reduce the need for exclusion from school, and to improve children's life chances;
•	Strengthen our response to children at risk of exclusion through commissioning and delivery of an evidence-led and trauma-informed menu of enhanced supports and interventions designed to reduce the need for children to be excluded from our schools and settings;  
•	Develop a holistic approach to inclusion and AP that reduces harms and poor outcomes, such as NEET, offending, missing from home, exploitation and abuse, drug dealing, substance misuse, serious youth violence and harmful sexual behaviour;
•	Apply a multi-disciplinary approach with expertise in safeguarding, youth justice, mental health, early help, family support, parenting, education, and health that joins up support pathways via assessment, planning, intervention and provision to reduce risks of exclusion and poor outcomes.
•	strengthening emotional health and well-being;
•	reducing offending rates of young people educated outside mainstream.

Mechanical & Electrical (M&E) Services including:
•	Heating, ventilation and air-conditioning;
•	Water supply, above and below ground surface and foul water drainage systems including pumps and petrol interceptors; sewerage treatment plants;
•	Control systems (BMS);
•	Power, including standby power systems;
•	Lighting (internal and external);
•	Lifts, hoists, man-safe and fall arrest systems;
•	Life systems, including fire alarms, extinguishers, dry risers, suppression systems and evacuation chairs;
•	Other items, e.g. automatic doors, power assisted doors and roller shutter doors;
•	Alarm systems: intruder alarms, panic alarms and screens, disabled WC alarms;
•	Water features.
Statutory test, inspection and/or monitoring including:
•	Water hygiene monitoring;
•	Portable appliance testing;
•	Periodic electrical testing;
•	Gas safety testing;
•	Emergency lighting testing;
•	Air quality monitoring.

These comprise public realm improvement works at the Forest Road Blackhorse Road E17 junction
as follows:
- Narrowing of the existing carriageway on all four approaches to the traffic signal junction to provide segregated cycle tracks and wider pedestrian footways.
- Use of granite paving to enhance the footways;
- Carriageway resurfacing;
- New traffic signalling infrastructure (installed by Transport for London) with associated civil
works.
- Alteration to highway drainage;
- Planting of new trees;
- Rationalising street furniture;
- New street furniture including additional cycle parking throughout area to help promote
sustainable travel.
